analysisWe need to produce an assert statement reflecting the comment.

We have method add(int index, E element). It inserts element at index, shifting elements right.

Comment: 
"If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then for every valid index from the insertion point to the old size of the list, the element at each subsequent index in the updated list is either null and was null in the old list, or is equal to the element at the previous index in the old list."

Interpretation: Let oldSize = \old(this.size()). The index is between 0 and oldSize inclusive (since insertion at size allowed). For all i such that index <= i < oldSize (i in that range), after addition, element at i+1 (since shift) equals either null (if old element at i was null) or equals old element at i. Actually statement: "element at each subsequent index in the updated list is either null and was null in the old list, or is equal to the element at the previous index in the old list." Means for each i from index to oldSize-1, the element at i+1 in new list is either null and old element at i was null, or equals old element at i. Since shift, the element at i+1 should be old element at i. The null case is covered because if old element was null, then equality holds (null equals null). But they explicitly mention "either null and was null in the old list". So we can express: for all int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . However need to handle null equality safe: use (this.get(i+1) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But spec language maybe simpler: this.get(i+1) == \old(this.get(i)). That already covers null case. But comment emphasises null case; we could express as equality.

Thus assert: index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . However need to handle null: we can use (this.get(i+1) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But many examples used .equals for objects; they didn't worry about null. In earlier example they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) . That would cause NullPointer if null. But they assume not null maybe. We'll follow same pattern.

Thus final assert:

assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

